    <ShortLegislativeSummaryEn>&lt;div&gt;A legislative summary is currently being prepared for this bill by the Parliamentary Information and Research Service of the Library of Parliament. Meanwhile, the following executive summary is available. &lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;On 11 June 2015, the Minister of Health introduced Bill C-70, An Act to amend the Controlled Drugs and Substances Act and to make related amendments to other Acts (Protection of Communities from the Evolving Dangerous Drug Trade Act), in the House of Commons and it was given first reading. &lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Bill C-70 amends the Controlled Drugs and Substances Act to, among other things, provide the Minister of Health with the authority to temporarily add potentially dangerous substances to a schedule to that Act in order to control them;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;•	expand the offence of possession, production, sale or importation so that it includes transport and applies to anything (including precursors) that is intended to be used in the production or trafficking of any controlled substance;&lt;br/&gt;•	streamline the disposition of seized, found or otherwise acquired controlled substances, precursors and chemical and non-chemical offence-related property;&lt;br/&gt;•	add an administrative monetary penalties scheme;&lt;br/&gt;•	modernize inspection powers;&lt;br/&gt;•	provide the Minister of Health with the authority to require a person who is authorized to conduct activities in relation to controlled substances or precursors to provide him or her with information respecting those activities; and&lt;br/&gt;•	expand the regulation-making authority respecting information so that it applies to the collection, use, retention, disclosure and disposal of information.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;It also makes related amendments to the Criminal Code and the Seized Property Management Act.&lt;/div&gt;</ShortLegislativeSummaryEn>
    <ShortLegislativeSummaryFr>&lt;div&gt;Le Service d'information et de recherche parlementaires de la Bibliothèque du Parlement rédige actuellement le résumé législatif de ce projet de loi.  Entre-temps, le Service met à votre disposition le sommaire suivant.  &lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Le 11 juin 2015, le Ministre de la santé a déposé le projet de loi C-70, Loi modifiant la Loi réglementant certaines drogues et autres substances et apportant des modifications connexes à d'autres lois (Loi visant la protection des collectivités contre le commerce en évolution des drogues dangereuses), à la Chambre des communes en première lecture. &lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Le projet de loi C-70 modifie la Loi réglementant certaines drogues et autres substances afin, notamment : &lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;•	de donner au ministre de la Santé le pouvoir d'ajouter temporairement une substance qui est potentiellement dangereuse à une annexe de cette loi, afin de pouvoir la réglementer;&lt;br/&gt;•	d'élargir l'infraction de possession, de production, de vente ou d'importation afin qu'elle vise aussi le transport et qu'elle s'applique à toute chose (notamment aux précurseurs) destinée à être utilisée pour la production ou le trafic d'une substance désignée;&lt;br/&gt;•	de simplifier la disposition des substances désignées, des précurseurs et des biens infractionnels chimiques ou non-chimiques qui ont été saisis, trouvés ou obtenus de toute autre manière;&lt;br/&gt;•	d'ajouter un régime de sanctions administratives pécuniaires;&lt;br/&gt;•	de moderniser les pouvoirs d'inspection;&lt;br/&gt;•	de donner au ministre de la Santé le pouvoir de requérir d'une personne qui est autorisée à exercer des activités relatives à des substances désignées ou à des précurseurs de lui fournir des renseignements à l'égard de ces activités;&lt;br/&gt;•	d'élargir le pouvoir réglementaire relatif aux renseignements afin qu'il s'applique à la collecte, l'utilisation, la conservation, la communication et le retrait de ceux-ci.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Le texte apporte aussi des modifications connexes au Code criminel et à la Loi sur l'administration des biens saisis.&lt;/div&gt;</ShortLegislativeSummaryFr>
    <ShortLegislativeSummary>&lt;div&gt;A legislative summary is currently being prepared for this bill by the Parliamentary Information and Research Service of the Library of Parliament. Meanwhile, the following executive summary is available. &lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;On 11 June 2015, the Minister of Health introduced Bill C-70, An Act to amend the Controlled Drugs and Substances Act and to make related amendments to other Acts (Protection of Communities from the Evolving Dangerous Drug Trade Act), in the House of Commons and it was given first reading. &lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;Bill C-70 amends the Controlled Drugs and Substances Act to, among other things, provide the Minister of Health with the authority to temporarily add potentially dangerous substances to a schedule to that Act in order to control them;&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;•	expand the offence of possession, production, sale or importation so that it includes transport and applies to anything (including precursors) that is intended to be used in the production or trafficking of any controlled substance;&lt;br/&gt;•	streamline the disposition of seized, found or otherwise acquired controlled substances, precursors and chemical and non-chemical offence-related property;&lt;br/&gt;•	add an administrative monetary penalties scheme;&lt;br/&gt;•	modernize inspection powers;&lt;br/&gt;•	provide the Minister of Health with the authority to require a person who is authorized to conduct activities in relation to controlled substances or precursors to provide him or her with information respecting those activities; and&lt;br/&gt;•	expand the regulation-making authority respecting information so that it applies to the collection, use, retention, disclosure and disposal of information.&lt;br/&gt;&lt;br/&gt;It also makes related amendments to the Criminal Code and the Seized Property Management Act.&lt;/div&gt;</ShortLegislativeSummary>
